g simpler  but already pretty hard i find  for me that's the field of causal  representation learning  and by that i mean so if we go back to  the classical ai at the beginning  uh we were people were assuming that the  symbols are given think of a chess  program we know what are the chess  pieces what other possible moves  and people were assuming that  also the programs how to generate  intelligence based on these by  processing these symbols are provided by  humans so both is provided and let's see  how far we get  and that didn't take us very far then  machine learning came around  and so with machine learning so that the  advancing machine was not just that the  rules are learned but also at least uh  with most of modern machine learning  that also that the representations and  the symbols are automatically learned  now if we as i've argued before we have  to move towards when to move towards  causal learning and not just everything  based on statistical dependencies only  then uh if we look at most methods in  causality in a certain sense we are  going back to the symbolic stage because  they are usually pretty low dimensional  and we have someone gives us the symbols  and now we want to learn what are the  causal relationships and how do we  correct for confounding etc  so in a way we've  even if we can combine it with machine  learning it looks like we've taken a  step back so i think what we need in  that direction is we have to develop  this field of causal representation  learning so we want to learn both the  connections the mechanisms that connect  variables and also what are the  variables in the first place how do you  identify the useful variables in high  dimensional data so i think that's  going to be interesting because current  representation learning is really mostly  about just learning statistical  representations which are useful for  prediction but  maybe not much more and  ultimately i like this quotation of  convert logan's the austrian et